✅ 6. Demonstrate yield()

6.1 Definition
🔴 yield() is a static method of the Thread class.
🔴 It is used by the currently executing thread to voluntarily give up its current CPU time slice.
🔴 The thread moves from the Running state back to the Runnable state, allowing the scheduler to choose another runnable thread.

Method Signature
public static native void yield();

Important: yield() is a hint to the scheduler, not a command.

6.2 Why Do We Need yield()?

Suppose two threads are running:

Thread-A
Thread-B

Thread-A realizes that it has been using the CPU for a while and wants to give Thread-B a chance.

It can call:
Thread.yield();

Now the scheduler may switch to another runnable thread.

6.3 Syntax
Thread.yield();

6.4 How yield() Works Internally

Suppose Thread-A is executing.

Running (Thread-A)
        │
        ▼
 Thread.yield()
        │
        ▼
Runnable State
        │
        ▼
Scheduler Decides
   ┌─────────────┐
   ▼             ▼
Thread-A     Thread-B

Key Point:

The scheduler may:

Continue executing Thread-A.
Switch to Thread-B.
Switch to Thread-C.

There is no guarantee.

6.5 What Happens After Calling yield()?

The thread:

🔴 Gives up its current time slice.
🔴 Moves back to the Runnable state.
🔴 Competes with other runnable threads.
🔴 May immediately be selected again.
🔴 It is non-blocking in nature.




